  "tldr": "The paper characterizes the sharp minimax radius for testing a constant conditional average treatment effect when the propensity and baseline regression are separately Hölder-smooth and may be too rough for first-order product-rate theory. A profiled observed-data moment makes CATE variance coercive under overlap, a known-design higher-order projection statistic supplies a computable uniform-size upper bound without empirical Gram inversion, and legal composite-null Bernoulli mixtures must deliver the matching converse. The unknown rate, its phase boundaries, and any boundary logarithms remain the explicit open kernel; the known-propensity benchmark and a concrete observational upper envelope discipline the answer.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Existing constant-CATE tests establish asymptotic validity or directional power under nuisance-rate and alignment conditions, while the closest causal minimax theorem concerns CATE estimation and the closest sharp testing theorems concern direct regression or heteroskedasticity.",
    "niche": "The unresolved object is a same-class two-sided separation frontier under an unknown constant null, separately rough propensity and baseline functions, unrestricted bounded outcome laws, and exactly uniform random design.",
